{"id":"9000811b-a99a-4487-90e4-cd3633a2c406","object":"chat.completion","created":1785033574,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    candidates = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['removable'] and n['id'] != 'root' and n['symbol'] not in required]\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda nid: (-node_map[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    removed = []\\n    for nid in candidates:\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        retained.discard(nid)\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to produce a Python implementation for solve(case) that adheres to the task description. The task is about grammar-guided node reduction: given a tree of nodes with ids, parent, symbol, depth, removable flag, utility, and a list of required_symbols, we need to decide which nodes to remove and which to retain, following rules: never remove root, required symbols, nodes with retained children; tentative removal only if all required symbols remain; deeper nodes before shallower; use node ID as tie break at equal depth; return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order.\n\nThe output format is {\"removed_node_ids\": [...], \"retained_node_ids\": [...]}. The list must be sorted? The schema says items type string, no ordering requirement mentioned but the description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" For retained_node_ids, they want sorted. For removed_node_ids, they want actual removal order (the order in which nodes were removed, which would be the order we process them? Probably the order we decide to remove them, i.e., the order in which we tentatively remove them. Since we process nodes in a specific order (depth descending, then id ascending), that would be the removal order. However, we need to be careful: we might remove a node only to later find that its removal is invalid because it causes loss of required symbols? The rule says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That implies we check before removal. So we iterate nodes in order, for each removable node, if after removing it the set of symbols of retained nodes still covers required_symbols, then we remove it. So the order of removal is the order we encounter nodes. That order must be consistent: deeper nodes first, then shallower, and tie-break by id ascending. So we need to sort all nodes (except root, non-removable?) according to that order. But we only consider nodes that are removable? The description says \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" It doesn't say we must only consider removable nodes? But the node has \"removable\" field. If a node is not removable, we cannot remove it regardless. So we only process removable nodes. Also, we must never remove root (id \"root\"? But root also has removable=false). So we can filter: nodes with removable=True, and not root (but root is not removable anyway). Also, we must never remove required symbols? Actually it says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" A node with retained children means if we remove a parent, but its children might be retained? Actually we are removing nodes; if we remove a node, we also remove its subtree? The problem statement: \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". This is typical in Perses: removal of a node means removing that node and its descendants. So when we remove a node, all its descendants are also removed. But the rule \"node with retained children\" means we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained? Actually \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That implies if a node's child is being retained (i.e., we decide to keep that child), we cannot remove the parent. But in the removal process, we might remove children first, so later the parent might have no children retained and then can be removed. So the order matters: deeper nodes first, because removing a leaf has no effect on its parent's children? Actually if we remove a leaf, its parent no longer has that child, but the parent might still have other children. The rule \"node with retained children\" means we cannot remove a parent if any of its children are still retained. So we must process from leaves upward: after removing some children, the parent may become removable. The description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we process nodes in decreasing depth order. That ensures that when we consider a node, all its descendants have already been considered (and possibly removed). So if we process in descending depth order, then when we get to a parent, its children have already been processed. If any child is retained (not removed), then the parent cannot be removed because it would remove that retained child. So the condition \"node with retained children\" is automatically enforced if we only consider nodes that are removable and check that after removal, all required symbols are still present. But we also need to check that the node has no retained children? Actually the spec says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That is an additional constraint beyond required symbols. So we must explicitly check: before removing a node, ensure that none of its children (direct children? or descendants?) are in the retained set. Since we process from leaves up, children are already decided (removed or retained). So we can check: if any child of this node is currently in the retained set (i.e., not removed yet), then we cannot remove this node. But note: if a child is removed, then it's not retained. So we need to keep track of which nodes are retained as we go. Initially, all nodes are retained (we haven't removed any). As we remove nodes, we move them from retained to removed. So the retained set shrinks. So for a node, if all its children are already removed (i.e., not in retained), then it has no retained children. That condition is necessary.\n\nAlso, we must never remove a required symbol node. But note: a node's symbol may be in required_symbols. So we must check that as well: if node.symbol in required_symbols, we cannot remove it.\n\nNow, the algorithm:\n\n1. Build a map from node id to node object.\n2. Build a map of parent -> children ids, and also child -> parent.\n3. Determine the order to process nodes: list of node ids that are removable and not root and not a required symbol. But wait, we should still consider required symbols? They are not removable. So we can filter initially: nodes that are removable and not root and symbol not in required_symbols. However, a required symbol node might have removable=True? In the examples, root is not removable, required_symbols \"root\" and \"s1\", but node n1 has removable=False and symbol \"s1\", so it's not removable. In general, required symbol nodes should have removable=False per spec? The spec says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\", so even if it were removable, we should not remove it. So we can filter out those nodes upfront.\n\n4. Sort the candidate node ids by depth descending, then by id ascending (as string? IDs are strings, can compare lexicographically). Since depth is descending, we want -depth, then id. Or use key=lambda nid: (-nodes[nid]['depth'], nid).\n\n5.